t back  and and and judge her for the choices  that she makes it occurred to me that  that that would that could be a  technique that we used and and they they  responded to it right away I think they  liked the idea you know I think they  came to me with this project because  because I experiment  and because because I take creative  risks you know visually so you know I  think that they were excited that I was  that I was excited for her I really  enjoyed that hospital shot where you  know she's sort of floating right I  don't want to say why yeah how did you  come up with that shot and talk a little  bit about filming it well yeah we we we  refer to it as a kind of infinite zoom I  was trying to design in this film the  whole way through a subjective  experience and she gets this news that  that her grandmother has had a stroke  who's raised her and she goes to the  hospital to see her and you know who  among us in a moment of crisis can  really remember getting from one place  to another right you just that you go  into this crisis mode and I think that  that I wanted to represent that with her  which is she's lost in thought and she's  not paying any attention to what's  happening around her and it had to be an  emotional moment as well which is why we  used the song and and the music in in  that spot to create an experience for  the audience where it's not literal but  it's it's magical sure I'm always  curious about the editing the the  editing room because it's the final  rewrite it's where everything comes  together so how long was your first cut  compared to the finished film you know  it wasn't that much longer I think it  was about maybe 10 minutes longer so  yeah it wasn't it wasn't bad and you  know I mean I i I'm an ensemble  storyteller and you know I would say  with those 10 minutes ended up being  were you know were point-of-view shifts  that ultimately in in the movie was not  where the audience wanted to be so you  know so really it it changed a 